@inject ApiMetadata Api

@* FilterForm's Overview, written by hand after Table's and the selection Overviews: the three
   filter fields (previewed in each mode), dates, filtering on demand, with a table, your own inputs,
   saying how many match, and its own words. Parts, Accessibility and See also come from the
   metadata and the doc comments; See also adds the order list demo. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A filter form is a row of filter fields above a list, such as a search box and a status select. It's a GET form: its values go in the URL, so a filtered view can be bookmarked, shared and reloaded, and it works <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">with JavaScript off</Anchor>. The form doesn't filter anything itself: the page reads the values with <Code>[SupplyParameterFromQuery]</Code> and filters its own data.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>To filter a table by its columns instead, use the <Anchor Href="docs/components/table">Table</Anchor>'s own column filters.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Search, select and checkbox</Heading>
<TextBlock>A filter form has three kinds of plain filter field. Add a <Code>FilterSearch</Code> for a search box, a <Code>FilterSelect</Code> for a choice with "All" first, and a <Code>FilterCheckbox</Code> for a filter that's on or off, such as <Code>?mine=true</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="filter-form-issues" />
<List>
    <ListItem>A <Code>FilterSearch</Code> writes <Code>?q=</Code> unless you set its <Code>Name</Code>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>With <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">IzzyBlazor's script</Anchor>, the search applies after a pause in typing, and the other fields as they change. Each applies without a full reload, and focus stays where it was.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A <Code>FilterSelect</Code>'s options come from <Code>Items</Code>, or from an enum set in <Code>EnumType</Code>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>The URL stores each option's value. To show the user other text, set <Code>ItemText</Code>.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Dates</Heading>
<TextBlock>A date filter narrows the list to a date, or a date and time. Add a <Code>FilterDate</Code>; for a date and time, set <Code>Type="DateFieldType.DateTime"</Code>. The page reads the value as a <Code>DateOnly?</Code> or <Code>DateTime?</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="filter-form-dates" />
<List>
    <ListItem>The URL stores the value the browser sends, such as <Code>2027-03-01</Code> or <Code>2027-03-01T14:30</Code>, with no time zone.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>The page decides which zone a time is in. Name the zone in the <Code>Label</Code>, as in "From (UTC)".</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Code>Min</Code> and <Code>Max</Code> limit the dates. This example sets the end date's <Code>Min</Code> from the start date.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A date applies a moment after it changes, so typing one part at a time applies once.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Filtering on demand</Heading>
<TextBlock>An on-demand form applies nothing until the user presses Apply or Enter. It suits filters that are slow to apply, such as a search against a large database, or forms where the user sets several filters before looking at the results. To keep the Apply button with IzzyBlazor's script too, set <Code>Live="false"</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="filter-form-apply-button" />
<TextBlock>To rename the button, set <Code>ApplyText</Code>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">With a table</Heading>
<TextBlock>A filter form above a <Anchor Href="docs/components/table">Table</Anchor> filters the rows by values that aren't tied to a column. Give the table the filtered rows. The form keeps the rest of the URL, such as the table's sort, and removes the page number, so filtering goes back to page 1.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="filter-form-books" />
<TextBlock>The form removes a table's page number with or without a <Code>QueryPrefix</Code>, such as <Code>books-page</Code>. The <Anchor Href="demos/filter-form/order-list">order list demo</Anchor> does the same for a whole page.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Custom inputs</Heading>
<TextBlock>A filter form can contain inputs of your own, such as a number. Put any input with a <Code>name</Code> in the form, and list the names in <Code>Fields</Code>, so the form doesn't also carry their old values over from the URL.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@OwnFields</Code>
<TextBlock>With IzzyBlazor's script, each input applies as a filter field of its kind does:</TextBlock>
<List>
    <ListItem>A text, search, email, phone or URL input applies after a pause in typing, like <Code>FilterSearch</Code>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A select or checkbox applies when it changes.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>Any other input, such as a number, applies a moment after it changes.</ListItem>
</List>
<TextBlock>Give each input a label, such as an <Code>aria-label</Code>; the form doesn't add one.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Announcing the number of results</Heading>
<TextBlock>Screen readers don't announce new results. Show the count next to the list in a live region (<Code>aria-live="polite"</Code>), as the examples do; screen readers read it each time it changes.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes, labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>To match a compact toolbar, set <Code>Size</Code> on each filter field; the buttons keep their own size.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>The form has a name for screen readers, and its buttons have text of their own. To set them for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>Label</Code></td><td>The search landmark's name</td><td>"Filters"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>ApplyText</Code></td><td>The Apply button's text</td><td>"Apply"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>ClearText</Code></td><td>The Clear link's text</td><td>"Clear"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>
<TextBlock>Each filter field's <Code>Label</Code> works differently:</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th><Code>Label</Code></th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>FilterSearch</Code></td><td>Optional and hidden; names the box for screen readers. Defaults to "Search".</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>FilterSelect</Code></td><td>Required and hidden; names the select. The first option reads "Label: All" unless you set <Code>AllText</Code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>FilterCheckbox</Code></td><td>Required; the checkbox's visible text.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>FilterDate</Code></td><td>Required; shown before the date.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

@if (OverviewParts.Of(Component, Api).Count > 0)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Parts</Heading>
}
<OverviewParts Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <FilterForm Label="Filter customers">
            <FilterSearch Placeholder="Search customers" />
            <FilterSelect Name="status" Label="Status" EnumType="typeof(CustomerStatus)" />
        </FilterForm>

        @code {
            // The page reads the filters from the address and filters its own data.
            [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "q")] private string? Search { get; set; }
            [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "status")] private string? Status { get; set; }
        }
        """;

    private const string OwnFields = """
        <FilterForm Fields="@(["over"])">
            <FilterSearch Placeholder="Search invoices" />
            <input type="number" name="over" value="@Over" min="0" aria-label="Over (USD)" />
        </FilterForm>

        @code {
            [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "over")] private decimal? Over { get; set; }
        }
        """;
}
